<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Campeche</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Campeche is a state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> on the western side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán Peninsula</a>, facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>. Its interior holds major Maya sites such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a> and Edzná, and its capital, the walled colonial port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco-de-campeche">San Francisco de Campeche</a>, gave the state its name. It was separated from Yucatán in 1857 and recognised as a state in 1863.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/campeche.jpg" alt="Campeche" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: (WT-en) Lahoriali at English Wikivoyage · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calakmul_bio_reserve.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It lay close to the San Pedro River, which flowed westward from the Petén, and commanded a north–south overland route linking southern <b>Campeche</b> with the Petén; its position between the dominant capitals Calakmul and Tikal added to its commercial weight.</blockquote><a href="/empire/el-peru-waka">El Perú-Waka' · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>At sea the Texas Navy's Austin and Wharton under Commodore Edwin Ward Moore fought the Mexican steamers inconclusively at the Naval Battle of <b>Campeche</b> in 1843 beside Yucatán.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-texas">Republic of Texas · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Popol Vuh belongs to the Kʼicheʼ people of Guatemala, one of the Maya peoples, who also live in the Mexican states of Chiapas, <b>Campeche</b>, Yucatan and Quintana Roo and in parts of Belize, Honduras and El Salvador.</blockquote><a href="/work/popol-vuh">Popol Vuh · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fall of the League of Mayapan around 1441, they settled in northern <b>Campeche</b> and established a province with Calkiní as its leading town.</blockquote><a href="/state/ah-canul">Ah Canul</a></li><li><blockquote>The province was absorbed into the Province of Yucatán, and the town survives in <b>Campeche</b> state.</blockquote><a href="/state/champoton">Champotón</a></li><li><blockquote>Edzná occupied a flood-prone valley in <b>Campeche</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/edzna">Edzná</a></li></ul></section>
